# Tablet intercom (Oct 2026)

A full `intercom` tile is a page of stations, Mute and Hang up, in the editor's Other tiles. A call is WebRTC audio directly between the two ends. The add-on only tells them who is online and forwards the offer, answer and network candidates. There is no STUN or TURN server, so a browser that is not on the tablet's network (a phone away from home, or Home Assistant through Nabu Casa) can open the page and the call will not connect.

## Signaling

The hub is in-process (`server/.../intercom/SignalingHub.kt`), shared by two listeners in `Application.kt`:

- `0.0.0.0:8766` WebSocket `/webrtc` for tablets, plus `GET /api/clients`. Published in `addon/config.yaml`.
- The ingress (editor) server's WebSocket `/webrtc`, for the Remote Screens panel. A page served by ingress cannot open port 8766.

Remote Screens does not open a socket to the WebRTC signaling server app. That app keeps port 8765 for the RPI tablets on `ws://ha.shafferco.com:8765/webrtc`. The message names match (`register`, `registered`, `offer`, `answer`, `candidate`, `hangup`, `unavailable`, `getClients`, `clientsList`, `heartbeat`, `replaced`, `error`, and app-level `ping` / `pong`), and the two rosters stay separate.

Both stations are marked in a call only when the target is online and not already in a call. Offline gets `unavailable` reason `offline` and stays on the roster as Online. Busy gets reason `busy`. `getClients` answers only the station that asked. The same `clientId` registering again gets `replaced` and the old socket is closed. A session idle for 90s is closed. An unknown session gets `{"type":"error","message":"not_registered"}`. A WebSocket ping goes out about every 30s, and a JSON `{"type":"ping","ts":…}` every 25s. `pong` only touches `lastActivity`.

## The page

`Tile.edgeToEdge()` and the tile dispatch send `intercom` to `render/panels/intercom/`. Taps `intercom_call`, `intercom_hangup` and `intercom_mute` go to `IntercomActions`, then `ServerMessage.IntercomCommand` back to that tablet only. They do not call Home Assistant.

The page matches RPI's `IntercomScreen`, `StationView` and `ButtonViewV2` on a wide landscape page. Colours are `IntercomPaint` (page `#494949`, column `#3B3B3B`, card `#2F2E2E`, accent `#1DA48C`, key `#444444`), not theme roles, so Day, Dark and OLED do not recolour it. The left column is 350dp: accent bar, INTERCOM, then the status and the two keys centred. Mute and Hang Up are side by side, 150×50, dark fill, 1dp black border. The glyph is 32dp so the caption still fits; RPI asks for 50dp, which covers the caption. Hang Up stays fully drawn while idle (RPI fades it until the call is connected). The English caption is "Hang Up". Each station is a 100dp card with an 8dp inset, the person icon, name, status, a Call key, and a 1dp accent line on the bottom. Call stays visible and has no action while this tablet is busy or that station is in a call.

That side-by-side page is used when the tile is at least 720dp wide and the scale against 1280×650 is at least 0.65 (`IntercomLayout`, mirrored by `editor/src/model/intercomLayout.ts`). A phone, or a landscape page shorter than that, stacks the same column as a header and scrolls the station list under it. The two keys stay on one row and shrink to the width. Captions stay 12sp only while the key is tall enough; on a smaller key they shrink with it. The editor mockup uses the same switch (`intercom-compact`, and `intercom-narrow` under 600dp).

Call state is on the player session (`PlayerSession.intercomReport`), not in `RenderDataRepository`. A station joining or leaving rebuilds the document. Mute, the status line, Online / In Call / Connected, and which key icon is showing update as document variables (`ic.status`, `ic.r{n}.status`, `ic.mic.on`, `ic.mic.off`, `ic.hang.live`, `ic.hang.idle`). A roster must not put the status back to Online after No answer, Call failed or Call ended. Only Connecting… and Online, while idle, are replaced.

A tablet registers only when its screen contains the tile. `ServerMessage.IntercomConfig` says so, sent with settings. The tablet opens `ws://<the host it uses for port 8100>:8766/webrtc`. Its client id is `station-` plus the full device id. Its display name is the tablet's name, or `Not Set` when that name is blank. Renaming re-registers.

The page is drawn before that registration. Its status starts as Connecting…. The tablet sends `intercom_report` when it is registered. A later reconnect builds a new document and sends `IntercomConfig` again; the signaling socket is often still up, so the tablet must publish that report again. Otherwise the page stays on Connecting… with an empty station list even though `/api/clients` already shows it.

An incoming offer is answered automatically, including the glare case where the lower client id wins, and wakes the screen. It does not change page. Hang up, a busy reply, a 30s timeout and a failed connection all send hangup, so the roster does not stick on In Call. While a call holds audio focus, this tablet's camera video stays off (`ViewState.isIntercomCall`).

An older player that did not advertise capability `intercom` still shows the page, with the status line telling you to update the tablet app. The add-on does not send it call commands.

The editor mockup is static (Living Room online, Kitchen in a call). It does not open a signaling socket.

## Calling from Home Assistant

A browser is another station. It registers as `ha-` plus a new id per open page.

- **Remote Screens panel.** The toolbar's Intercom button opens `IntercomDesk.vue`. Its socket is the ingress `/webrtc` route, same origin as the editor. The layout rail is still Add / Tree / Page / Screen; Intercom is not a rail tool. The panel's display name is "Home Assistant".
- **Any dashboard.** `custom:remote-screens-intercom`, served from `custom_components/remote_screens/frontend/` and registered in `async_setup`. With no options it lists the online stations. A `station` option pins one display name, so a card can be "Call Kitchen". The card's page cannot open port 8766, so `intercom.py` opens one WebSocket to `ws://<the add-on host>:8766/webrtc` per card session and forwards the frames over the Home Assistant WebSocket. The browser never sees 8766. The card's display name is the signed-in user.

The browser asks for the microphone when a call starts, and still answers if that request fails, so the tablet can be heard. The tablet answers on its own, so Call plays on that tablet's speaker.

## A call that sits on Calling

Verified 2026-10-25 on the USB tablet (M8L 2022, `adb` serial `4070306023037966`, device `bc3c86df-8e73-44d1-b502-36637279b58d`, LAN `10.11.0.89`). Its clock is EST, an hour behind the add-on. A call that never connects stays on Calling for 30s (`CALL_TIMEOUT_MS`) and then hangs up. That is the timeout, not a frozen UI.

Two mistakes caused it:

- `SessionAudioListener` drops a candidate when `peerId` is still null. Host candidates fire during `createOffer` / `acceptOffer`. `placeCall` and `onOffer` must set `peerId` before starting audio. With no STUN server those host addresses are the only ones the browser can use. Chrome's own candidates are `.local` mDNS names, which the tablet often cannot resolve.
- The browser's `getUserMedia` throws on plain HTTP (`http://10.11.0.17:8123` is not a secure context) and also when an incoming offer is not a click. `IntercomBrowser` and the Lovelace card must still answer, with a recvonly audio transceiver when there is no microphone. An exception that sends no answer leaves the tablet on Calling for the full 30s.

Candidates that arrive before `setRemoteDescription` are queued (`AndroidWebRtcAudio`, `rememberCandidate` in `intercomCall.ts` and the card) and applied after it. The browser waits up to 1.5s for ICE gathering and puts those candidates in the offer or answer, and does not trickle until that message has been sent. `crypto.randomUUID()` throws on the same HTTP page and blanks `IntercomDesk` during setup; ids use `crypto.getRandomValues`. The signaling URL appends `webrtc` under the page directory. `new URL('webrtc', href)` drops the ingress token when the path has no trailing slash.

On plain HTTP the computer can hear the tablet. Talking back needs a microphone, which the browser only allows on HTTPS. Same-LAN host candidates are enough once both sides exchange them. A phone off the LAN, or Home Assistant through Nabu Casa, still will not connect.

## The integration must import

`intercom.py` imports `CONF_HOST` from `homeassistant.const`, as `__init__.py` and `config_flow.py` do. It is not defined in `const.py`. Importing it from `.const` raises `ImportError` while the component loads, and the config entry stays `not_loaded`. The card's static path uses `homeassistant.components.http`, so `manifest.json` lists `http` in `after_dependencies`. Hassfest fails without that. The integration tests have no http component (`hass.http` is None); setup registers the websocket commands and skips the card in that case. Home Assistant caches that failed import until a core restart. Reloading the config entry does not clear it. `async_setup` (which calls `async_setup_intercom`) does not run again on a config-entry reload.

`intercom.py` and `frontend/intercom-card.js` live on the Samba `config` share (`config/custom_components/remote_screens/`), not in the add-on image. The panel's script is the editor bundle inside the add-on, so a panel change needs `tools/deploy_ha_dev.sh --no-rebuild` and a rebuild of `local_remote_screens`. Refresh the Home Assistant page after that rebuild. The store add-on `032995b0_remote_screens` stays stopped. Player changes need `./gradlew :player:assembleRelease` first (the script reuses an existing APK) and `adb install -r` on this tablet. The release APK is ARM only. The same `addon/VERSION` does not offer that APK to other tablets.
